@page "/versions"

<PageTitle>Versions and support · RioCharts for Blazor and .NET</PageTitle>

<h1>Versions and support</h1>
<p class="lead">
    From 1.0, RioCharts follows semantic versioning: updating within 1.x never breaks your code or your saved charts.
    The current packages are 1.0 release candidates: the API is frozen, and only fixes and non-breaking additions
    go in before 1.0.
</p>

<h2 id="promise">What 1.x keeps</h2>
<table class="docs">
    <tr><th>Kept in every 1.x</th><th>Details</th></tr>
    <tr>
        <td>The public API</td>
        <td>
            Every public type and member of RioCharts.Core, RioCharts.Blazor, RioCharts.Skia and RioCharts.AI: components,
            parameters, events, methods and the chart model. New members can arrive in any 1.x; none is removed or changes
            meaning before 2.0. The build checks this against a list of every public symbol.
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>JSON chart definitions</td>
        <td>
            A definition saved with any 1.x (<code>"version": 1</code>, or no version) renders in every later 1.x: fields, chart
            type names, their synonyms and option words can be added, never removed. See <a href="definitions#versions">Definitions</a>.
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>AI tools</td>
        <td>The tools of riocharts-mcp and <code>ChartTool</code>: their names and arguments.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>Theming</td>
        <td>The <code>--rio-*</code> CSS variables and <code>ThemeTokens</code> (see <a href="theming">Theming</a>).</td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 id="may-change">What a 1.x update may change</h2>
<ul>
    <li>
        How charts look: spacing, tick choices, label placement and other layout details improve in minor releases. Charts keep
        showing the same data; only the drawing gets better.
    </li>
    <li>The SVG markup and its <code>rio-*</code> class names. Style charts with the CSS variables, not with selectors on their elements.</li>
    <li>Texts the chart writes: tooltips, screen reader descriptions and announcements, warnings.</li>
    <li>Performance, and anything internal.</li>
</ul>
<p>Breaking changes wait for 2.0, and the <a href="https://github.com/joadan/RioCharts/blob/master/CHANGELOG.md">changelog</a> lists each change in every release.</p>

<h2 id="dotnet">.NET versions</h2>
<p>
    The packages target the current long-term support .NET and the newest .NET: today .NET 10 (LTS) and .NET 11.
    When a new .NET ships, a 1.x minor release adds it. A target is removed only after Microsoft ends its support, in a minor
    release that says so in the changelog. Your app keeps working on the version it has until you update .NET.
</p>
<p>
    Blazor runs where ASP.NET Core does: in the current versions of the common browsers, with Server, WebAssembly, Auto
    and static rendering. Server export with RioCharts.Skia runs on Windows, Linux and macOS.
</p>

<h2 id="fixes">Fixes</h2>
<p>
    Fixes go into the newest 1.x release; update within 1.x to get them. Report problems on
    <a href="https://github.com/joadan/RioCharts/issues">GitHub</a>.
</p>
